# Target C-II — coupled adaptive O–O path reaches `t<=1.8623`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8623`  
**Dependencies:** corrected joint-feasibility O–O path theorem; retained dominant O–O endpoint-count inequality; physical same-branch upper bound and Section-157 bracing; pairwise noncrossing dominant phase windows; exact laminar interval support; phase-free dual-path region saturation; diameter-path Helly hub; adjacent-strip reaction nondegeneracy  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_joint_coupled_adaptive_path_t18623.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The previous corrected path continuation stopped at `t<=1.86177`, with a reported continuous zero-mass endpoint near `1.861799`. That endpoint was not intrinsic to the joint-feasibility system. It came from combining two adverse substitutions which cannot generally occur in the same source:

1. retained dominant outer mass `z` was minimized on the low-outer-energy feasibility edge `delta=F(z)`;
2. the subsequent weighted-Markov estimate was evaluated at the unrelated global upper bound `delta=delta_max`.

The present note keeps `z` and `delta` coupled. A second improvement chooses the terminal-energy threshold from the source's actual outer energy instead of fixing it in advance.

At

\[
T={18623\over10000}=1.8623
\]

this gives the source-level normal form

\[
\boxed{P_{low}>0.00198-o(1)},
\]

hence one of the at most three dominant phase windows contains more than

\[
\boxed{0.00066D-o(D)}
\]

actual low-energy outer-to-outer complete stopped trains. Their physical terminal branches are opposite, so the selected crosscuts form one simple complement-dual path. Every path region is diameter-endpoint loaded, all actual diameter support intervals contain one common hub, every nonhub switch is one-sided toward that hub, and some nonhub strip has at most

\[
\boxed{9519}
\]

moving actual diameter rows. Its exact nonzero KKT layer reaction is generated by one or two actual switching diameter bars.

Thus the corrected path/hub/loaded-region/finite-strip architecture now overlaps the modern pentagon-sea endpoint `t<=1.8623` instead of stopping below it.

---

## 1. Corrected retained-mass feasibility

Keep the parent notation

\[
t={q-k\over D},\qquad \kappa={k\over D}\ge0,
\]

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-{\kappa\over2},
\]

and

\[
C_{76}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0076,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

At the endpoint `T`, put

\[
U_0={9T\over2}-2\pi.
\]

The matched atomic floor gives

\[
\delta\le {U_0-C_{76}\over2}+o(1).
\]

The exact certificate proves

\[
U_0<2.097165,
\qquad
\boxed{\delta<0.093+o(1).}
\tag{CA.1}
\]

Let

\[
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6).
\]

The corrected joint-feasibility theorem defines, after the adverse relaxation `t=T`, `kappa=0`,

\[
\bar U(\delta)=U_0-2\delta,
\]

\[
\bar S(\delta)
=\pi-\bar U(\delta)-{T\over2}+m_2(\bar U(\delta)),
\]

\[
\bar p(\delta,z)
={\delta-F(z)+r(\bar U(\delta))^2\over2(1-\delta)},
\]

where

\[
m_2(U)={U+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-U^2}\over2},
\qquad
r(U)=U-m_2(U),
\]

and

\[
G(\delta,z)=z+\bar p(\delta,z)-\bar S(\delta).
\tag{CA.2}
\]

Every actual survivor satisfies

\[
G(\delta,z)\ge-o(1),
\qquad
\delta\ge F(z)-o(1).
\tag{CA.3}
\]

The parent derivative calculation gives `G_delta<0`. Therefore

\[
H(z):=G(F(z),z)
\]

is an adverse one-variable feasibility envelope.

For

\[
z_0={5231\over2500}=2.0924,
\]

the exact outward certificate verifies the same monotonicity gates on `[0,z_0]` and obtains

\[
\boxed{H(z_0)<-5.59\times10^{-5}.}
\tag{CA.4}
\]

Hence every survivor in the stated scope has

\[
\boxed{z>2.0924-o(1).}
\tag{CA.5}
\]

Put

\[
d_0:=F(z_0).
\]

The certificate encloses

\[
d_0=0.04215403645\ldots .
\tag{CA.6}
\]

Since `F` is increasing on the relevant interval, (CA.3) and (CA.5) imply

\[
\delta\ge d_0-o(1).
\tag{CA.7}
\]

---

## 2. Feasibility forces `z` upward when `delta` rises

The exact parent identity is

\[
2(1-\delta)^2G_\delta
=
1-F(z)+r^2
-4r'(1-\delta)(r+1-\delta),
\tag{CA.8}
\]

with `r=r(\bar U(delta))`.

Throughout the present rectangle,

\[
r'>1,
\qquad F(z)\ge0,
\qquad r<1.05,
\qquad1-\delta>0.907.
\]

Also `r<4(1-delta)`. Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
2(1-\delta)^2G_\delta
&\le1+r^2-4(1-\delta)(r+1-\delta)\\
&=1-4(1-\delta)^2+r[r-4(1-\delta)]\\
&\le1-4(1-\delta)^2.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus

\[
G_\delta
\le{1\over2(1-\delta)^2}-2
<-1.39.
\tag{CA.9}
\]

At fixed `delta`,

\[
G_z
=1-{F'(z)\over2(1-\delta)}
\le1.
\tag{CA.10}
\]

Compare an actual feasible point `(delta,z)` with `(d_0,z_0)`. From (CA.9)–(CA.10),

\[
G(\delta,z)
\le H(z_0)+(z-z_0)-1.39(\delta-d_0).
\]

Use `G(delta,z)>=-o(1)` and the strict negative margin (CA.4). Dropping the favorable fixed margin gives

\[
\boxed{
z-z_0
\ge1.39(\delta-d_0)-o(1).
}
\tag{CA.11}

This is the coupling absent from the previous endpoint proof. A source cannot simultaneously use the minimum feasible `z` and the maximum allowed `delta`.

---

## 3. Source-adaptive terminal-energy threshold

For a train with terminal directional energy at most `lambda`, the audited same-oriented-branch estimate and Section-157 bracing give

\[
{r_\tau^2\over D^2}
\le\lambda- {\lambda^2\over4}+o(1),
\tag{CA.12}
\]

and

\[
{r_\tau\over D}
\ge1-\delta-{\lambda\over2}-o(1).
\tag{CA.13}
\]

The same-oriented branch is therefore impossible whenever

\[
\left(1-\delta-{\lambda\over2}\right)^2
>
\lambda- {\lambda^2\over4}.
\tag{CA.14}
\]

The smaller root of equality in `lambda` is

\[
\lambda_-(\delta)
=2-\delta-\sqrt{2-\delta^2}.
\tag{CA.15}
\]

For each finite source choose the common threshold

\[
\boxed{
\lambda(\delta)=0.5857-\delta.
}
\tag{CA.16}
\]

This is a selection parameter, not a new hypothesis. By (CA.1),

\[
\lambda(\delta)>0.4927-o(1).
\]

Moreover

\[
0.5857<2-\sqrt2,
\]

so

\[
\lambda(\delta)
<2-\delta-\sqrt2
<2-\delta-\sqrt{2-\delta^2}
=\lambda_-(\delta).
\tag{CA.17}
\]

Hence every train selected by (CA.16) has opposite oriented terminal branches.

The threshold is also uniformly below one. If the endpoint deviations from the two ideal opposite normals are `alpha,beta`, then each satisfies

\[
1-\cos\alpha\le e_\tau\le0.5857+o(1),
\]

and similarly for `beta`. Thus `alpha,beta` stay uniformly below `pi/2`. The two physical host arcs around the opposite ideal normals consequently remain disjoint with a fixed positive gap. No `lambda<1/2` shortcut is needed.

---

## 4. Coupled Markov bound

Let `P_OO^dom` be retained dominant O–O pair weight. The audited endpoint-count inequality gives

\[
P_{OO}^{dom}\ge z-U-o(1).
\]

Weighted Markov at the sourcewise threshold (CA.16) gives

\[
P_{low}
\ge P_{OO}^{dom}-{\delta\over\lambda(\delta)}-o(1).
\]

Using the definition of `U` and adversely setting `t=T`, `kappa=0`,

\[
P_{low}
\ge z-U_0+q(\delta)-o(1),
\tag{CA.18}
\]

where

\[
q(\delta)
:=\delta\left(2-{1\over0.5857-\delta}\right).
\tag{CA.19}
\]

Differentiate:

\[
q'(\delta)
=2-{1\over0.5857-\delta}
-{\delta\over(0.5857-\delta)^2}.
\]

On `delta<0.093`, the exact rational bounds give

\[
\boxed{q'(\delta)>-0.42.}
\tag{CA.20}
\]

Combine (CA.11), (CA.18), and (CA.20). For `delta>=d_0`, the right side of

\[
P_{low}
\ge z_0-U_0
+1.39(\delta-d_0)+q(\delta)-o(1)
\]

has derivative greater than

\[
1.39-0.42=0.97.
\]

It is therefore minimized at `delta=d_0`, not at `delta=delta_max`. Hence

\[
P_{low}
\ge z_0-U_0+q(d_0)-o(1).
\tag{CA.21}
\]

The exact certificate proves

\[
q(d_0)>0.00675430
\]

and finally

\[
\boxed{
P_{low}>0.0019896-o(1)>0.00198-o(1).
}
\tag{CA.22}

This is the key repaired estimate.

---

## 5. A linear same-window O–O dual path

The dominant atomic orbit has at most three shrinking projective phase windows. Partition the retained low-energy pair weight among them. One window therefore carries more than

\[
{0.00198\over3}D-o(D)
=
\boxed{0.00066D-o(D)}.
\tag{CA.23}

Retained pair weight per complete train is at most one, so that same window contains more than `0.00066D-o(D)` actual O–O complete stopped trains.

After freezing sufficiently small windows, trains in one dominant window are pairwise noncrossing. Section 3 places all selected terminal pairs on the same two separated opposite physical boundary arcs. Pairwise disjoint proper crosscuts joining two disjoint boundary arcs are totally ordered. Therefore the selected family has one simple complement-dual path

\[
R_0\xleftrightarrow{\tau_1}R_1
\xleftrightarrow{}\cdots
\xleftrightarrow{\tau_m}R_m,
\]

with

\[
\boxed{m>0.00066D-o(D).}
\tag{CA.24}

The hostile-audited phase-free path-saturation theorem applies directly: every one of the `m+1` path regions contains an endpoint of an actual diameter edge.

The diameter-path Helly theorem supplies one common routing region `R_*` on every actual diameter support interval. Hence every nonhub switching interface is one-sided toward the hub.

---

## 6. Finite nonhub strip

Let `N_i` be the number of actual diameter support intervals starting or stopping at internal path boundary `i`. Exact interval support gives

\[
\sum_iN_i\le2|H|.
\]

Hopf–Pannwitz and the force-hull perimeter bound give

\[
|H|\le\pi D+o(D).
\]

Delete the unique hub boundary. By (CA.24), more than `0.00066D-o(D)` internal boundaries remain asymptotically. Using `pi<355/113`,

\[
{2(355/113)\over0.00066}
=9519.97\ldots<9520.
\]

Therefore, for every sufficiently large source, some nonhub strip satisfies

\[
\boxed{N_i\le9519.}
\tag{CA.25}

All switching diameter bars on this strip point topologically toward the hub side. The exact strip-reaction nondegeneracy theorem gives a nonzero KKT layer reaction, and planar conic Carathéodory reduces that reaction to one or two actual switching diameter bars.

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The live path normal form is now

```text
1.844 < t <= 1.8623:
  retained dominant outer mass z > 2.0924;
  >0.00198 D adaptively selected low-energy dominant O-O pair weight;
  >0.00066 D same-window O-O path trains;
  physical opposite terminal branches on two separated arcs;
  every dual path region contains an actual diameter endpoint;
  every actual diameter support interval contains one common hub;
  every nonhub switch is one-sided toward that hub;
  some nonhub strip has <=9519 moving actual diameter rows;
  exact nonzero strip reaction generated by <=2 actual bars.
```

This strictly supersedes the `1.86177` endpoint of the rectangular-extrema continuation. It also reaches the first modern pentagon-sea endpoint exactly, so the O–O path structure and the dense source-empty pentagon/flower structure can now be imposed simultaneously at `t<=1.8623`.

A floating-point exploration of the same coupled/adaptive system remains positive somewhat above `1.8623`; no higher endpoint is claimed here until separately outward-certified.

No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only coupled adaptive path/hub/region-saturation/finite-strip normal form through `t<=1.8623`. C-II and Level I remain open.
